er at pages 541-542, the packing trade at page 389, the fire department at pages 207-208, the Kinzie-Clybourn genealogy quoted from Blanchard at page 35, and the Biographical sketches of P. F. W. Peck, Philo Carpenter, John Dean Caton and Alexander N. Fullerton at pages 570-577 and 584; and, for the shore of 1812, the chapter 'The Massacre' at pages 49-50, 54 and 60-61 - the low, discontinuous sand-hills south of Twelfth Street, the scattered cottonwoods on the bank's lower reaches, and the two 'Massacre trees' of Eighteenth Street with Mrs. Kinzie's word that they were saplings in 1812.","counts":{"claims":206,"entities":77,"grades":[[131,49,26],[45,23,9]]},"date":"1895","has_archive_link":true,"source_id":"moses_kirkland_history_of_chicago_v1","tier":3,"type":"book","use":"scene"},{"citation":"John Moses and Joseph Kirkland (eds.), History of Chicago, Illinois, vol. 2 (Chicago and New York: Munsell & Co., 1895). Read here for the town before 1836: the Chicago Democrat's second office in Chapter I, 'The Press'; the school section sale and Granville T. Sproat's school in the education chapter at printed page 103 and before it; Cook county's first term of court, Richard J. Hamilton's house and the Caton & Collins partnership in 'The Bench and Bar' at pages 150-157; the first and second drug stores in the medical chapter; the first Sunday school, the first Baptist and Episcopal organizations, Tippecanoe Hall and Father Walker's cabin in the church chapters at pages 294, 348-349, 367 and 374-375; the Miller brothers' tannery, Blodgett's brick yard and the Jones & King hardware firm in the manufactures chapters at pages 406-407, 441 and 450-456; and Dr. Temple's building and stage line in the memoirs after page 624.","counts":{"claims":31,"entities":26,"grades":[[6,13,12],[5,9,12]]},"date":"1895","has_archive_link":true,"source_id":"moses_kirkland_history_of_chicago_v2","tier":3,"type":"book","use":"scene"},{"citation":"William Sidney Mount, Bar-room Scene,1835, Art Ins